<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1440">1440</a>–<a href="/years/1505">1505</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan III was Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in the late fifteenth century, often called Ivan the Great. He unified much of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> under Moscow, annexed Novgorod and ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship after the stand on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> in 1480. His reign laid the foundations of the Russian state that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-iii.jpg" alt="Ivan III" width="316" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_III_of_Russia_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Vasilyevich was born on 22 January 1440, the son of Vasily II, grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a>. He served as co-ruler and regent for his blind father before he officially ascended the throne in 1462. Vasily II's will gave him the grand principality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ivan's rule was marked by a vast expansion of Muscovite territory. After a war with the Novgorod Republic in 1471, when his army defeated the Novgorodians at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shelon">Battle of Shelon</a> on 14 July, Novgorod ceded much of its northern territory and paid an indemnity of 15,500 rubles. In 1478 it recognised his direct rule, and he abolished its veche. Its acquisition nearly doubled the size of his realm. Other principalities were absorbed by conquest, purchase or marriage contract: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> in 1463, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> in 1474, Tver in 1485 and Vyatka in 1489. His last will ordered that the domains of all his kinsfolk pass to the reigning grand prince after their deaths instead of to their heirs.</p><p>In 1476 Ivan refused to pay the customary tribute to Ahmed Khan of the Great Horde. In 1480 Ahmed invaded, and the Muscovite and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> armies faced each other across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> until Ahmed retreated into the steppe on 11 November. Russian historiography marks this as the end of the Tatar yoke. In 1500 Ivan defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> at the Battle of Vedrosha, and in 1503 Grand Duke Alexander ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, Starodub, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novhorod-siverskyi">Novgorod-Seversky</a> and sixteen other towns for peace.</p><p>After the death of Maria of Tver in 1467 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-palaiologina">Sophia Palaiologina</a> in 1472, a niece of the last Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-xi-palaiologos">Constantine XI</a>. The marriage was sponsored by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> in the hope of binding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, but she endorsed Orthodoxy. Under her influence the double-headed eagle was adopted in Moscow. From 1484 Ivan used the title tsar in foreign correspondence. He reduced the boyars' powers, granted estates called pomestie in return for military service, and had a new law code, the sudebnik, compiled; it restricted the movement of peasants and so laid the groundwork for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ivan died on 27 October 1505 and was succeeded by his son Vasily III. Moscow's territory had grown from under 600 square miles under Ivan II to more than 15,000 at the end of Vasily II's reign, and Ivan III absorbed its old rivals. Russian historians call him the gatherer of the Russian lands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan III of Russia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moscow under <b>Ivan III</b> stopped paying tribute, and the Great Stand on the Ugra River in 1480 ended without battle when Khan Ahmed withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Ivan III</b> the army grew larger and better organised.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Territorial states grew stronger and less willing to tolerate foreign privileges: <b>Ivan III of Moscow</b> closed the Novgorod office in 1494, Sweden under Gustav Vasa and Denmark reduced Lübeck’s influence after the Count’s Feud, and Elizabeth I of England revoked the privileges of the London Steelyard in 1598.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod’s militia proved no match for Moscow’s armies: Vasily II defeated it at Staraya Russa in 1456, and <b>Ivan III</b>’s commanders routed it on the Shelon River in 1471.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The closure of the Hanseatic Peterhof at Novgorod by <b>Ivan III of Moscow</b> in 1494 disrupted trade, though Reval and the other Livonian towns continued to profit as intermediaries for Western merchants dealing with Russia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its foundations, however, had been laid across the preceding two centuries of Muscovite expansion, beginning with <b>Ivan III</b>'s repudiation of Mongol suzerainty in 1480 and the gradual absorption of rival Russian principalities into the Grand Duchy of Moscow.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1482" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ivan III"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1482">What was here in 1482? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
